One of the most common questions in Kubernetes security is:
 👉 What’s the difference between a Role and a ClusterRole?

 Quick Comparison:
 🔹 Role → Single namespace → bound with RoleBinding
 🔹 ClusterRole → Cluster-wide → bound with ClusterRoleBinding (or    RoleBinding if namespace-scoped)

Use Role for: Namespace-specific tasks (e.g., reading Pods in default)
Use ClusterRole for: Cluster-level resources (e.g., Nodes, Persistent Volumes)

⚙️ How permissions are defined:
 Rules include:
verbs → get, list, create, delete
resources → pods, deployments, services
apiGroups → "" (core APIs), "apps", etc.

📌 Example Rule:
rules:
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["create", "delete", "update"]

Tip: Use Role whenever possible (namespace-scoped), and only use ClusterRole when access needs to span multiple namespaces or cluster-wide resources.

🛡️ Security Best Practices:
Principle of Least Privilege → Grant only required permissions
Audit regularly → Monitor access and detect misconfigurations
Namespace isolation → Prefer Roles over ClusterRoles
ServiceAccounts first → Use them over users for automation
Review & rotate → Periodically audit bindings
